WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.710
From his role as a part of Biff's gang in
multiple Back to the Future movies, to wicked

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:10.660
Cal in Titanic, Billy Zane was once a reliably
dashing presence on screens both big and small.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.959
He doesn't seem to pop up to provide his quirky
charm as much anymore, however, and we've

00:00:14.959 --> 00:00:16.790
wondered what he's been up to recently.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:22.700
Zane has apparently been in over 20 different
projects since 2014, and has an army of dedicated

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:23.700
fans…

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.750
"What's it like knowing you've got an army
of Zane-heads around the world?

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:27.750
Zane-meisters?"

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.740
"I believe they're called 'Zaniacs,' if I'm
not mistaken."

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.460
… so why aren't we actually seeing him?

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.340
Here's why you don't see Billy Zane much anymore…and
how that could change.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.780
He's not a leading man

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:44.280
Zane scored a series of hits with supporting
appearances in projects like Titanic, Dead

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.700
Calm, and Charmed.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.960
But on the handful of occasions in which he
was the lead actor in a big-budget Hollywood

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.680
movie, he tended to strike out.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.000
Take, for example, the flashy 1996 superhero
movie The Phantom.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.320
Properties from the 1930s might not be the
best things to adapt to the big screen — which

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.110
we're reminded of again and again with films
like The Green Hornet and The Lone Ranger

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.890
and The Shadow — so it's probably not really
Zane's fault.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:13.670
The Phantom was intended to be the start of
a franchise, but it made just $17 million

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:15.000
at the box office.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.031
That franchise never materialized, and while
the part got James Cameron's attention, it

00:01:19.031 --> 00:01:21.580
didn't land Zane a leading role.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:23.580
He can't escape Titanic

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.670
Anytime Zane makes the rounds to promote a
new project, interviewers don't want to talk

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:28.670
about it.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:30.369
All they want to discuss is Titanic.

00:01:30.369 --> 00:01:34.830
Sure, it was once the highest-grossing movie
of all time and spent more than a dozen weeks

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:39.159
as the #1 movie at the box office, and he
of course played the villainous, controlling

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:41.500
Cal expertly — but it's time to move on,
people.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.840
"Is it annoying that people love the movie
like they do?"

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:44.460
"Yeah, I hate it.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.180
It's awful, really, no.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:47.280
You know."

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:48.480
"I'm curious…"

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:49.640
"It's the gift that keeps giving.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:50.600
It's such a great honor."

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.600
He's made it clear that he's aware of how
lucky he was to be in one of the most popular

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.320
movies of all time, and bears the overwhelming
Titanic phenomenon no ill will.

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:02.579
But it must be tough to get out of the big
boat's shadow.

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:05.119
His TV series are flops

00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:10.310
In the summer of 2016, Zane had one of his
highest-profile gigs in years with the soap

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:15.200
Guilt, in which Zane played Stan Gutterie,
a fashionable, scheming defense lawyer who

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.620
has to navigate a foreign legal system.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.370
The series was canceled after one short season.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:25.090
Zane even played an important role in the
Amazon original series Mad Dogs, as the quirky,

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:30.480
roguish Milo, a part most definitely in his
wheelhouse… even if, spoiler alert, he dies

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:31.930
in the first episode.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:36.480
Nevertheless, scarcely a month after its debut,
Amazon announced there would be no second

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.209
season, barring any ghostly Billy Zane appearances
or flashbacks from ever happening.

00:02:41.209 --> 00:02:46.940
Back in 2010, Zane even seemed to have a promising
role in ABC's legal drama, The Deep End, but

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:50.790
that show was killed prematurely also, after
just six episodes.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:56.130
Zane also had a recurring role in the 2009
ABC sitcom Samantha Who?, which was also put

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.790
out of its misery midway through its second
season.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:03.160
To put it simply, Zane's TV efforts have always
been doomed, but it's not like he doesn't

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.600
have other projects going on.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.960
They're just a little less visible.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.110
He makes his own movies

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:13.630
Zane might not always be in front of the camera,
but he stays active in movie-making as a principal

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:18.280
partner in a production company and content
distributor called RadioactiveGiant.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.610
The company's produced a handful of modest
movies, including Killing Winston Jones and

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.830
the acclaimed documentary, Searching for Eddie
Running Wolf.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:29.830
And back in the late '90s, Zane produced and
starred in a labor of love he produced called

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:35.140
I Woke Up Early the Day I Died, a silent film
based on an unproduced screenplay left behind

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:38.300
by famed bad moviemaker Ed Wood.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.220
He's busy selling chicken

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:45.510
Nothing catches fast-food customers' attention
quite like a change in a chain's beloved mascot,

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.390
and KFC has taken full advantage of this over
the last few years.

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:54.600
After decades of existing mainly as a kindly,
kind of greasy cartoon voiced by Randy Quaid,

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:59.290
a real life Colonel Sanders started appearing
in KFC's commercials again — but with a

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:00.290
twist.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:04.050
Instead of an anonymous actor playing the
part, KFC began using well-known faces like

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:08.340
SNL vet Norm Macdonald and stand-up comic
Jim Gaffigan.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:13.390
Zane joined the Colonel Sanders lineup in
early 2017, slathering himself in gold paint

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:16.509
to help roll out the restaurant's line of
"Georgia Gold Chicken."

00:04:16.509 --> 00:04:21.009
"I put gold on my legs, my thighs, my tenders."

00:04:21.009 --> 00:04:26.199
As KFC CMO Kevin Hochman explained, the idea
to hire Zane was directly inspired by his

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:30.949
past as a villainous rich dude, which is supposed
to underscore the idea that anyone can dig

00:04:30.949 --> 00:04:33.039
into a bag of drive-through poultry.

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:36.020
If it's good enough for Billy Zane, it's good
enough for us.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:37.889
"That's good chicken."

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:38.889
"Thanks!"

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:41.699
It's not the first time Zane has been in a
food commercial, though….

00:04:41.699 --> 00:04:43.439
"Big big big big cookie time.

00:04:43.439 --> 00:04:45.080
Big big big big cookie time.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:46.220
Archman cookies!

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:48.320
Wouldn't you like some now?"

00:04:49.089 --> 00:04:51.780
… but it's definitely the least embarrassing.

00:04:51.789 --> 00:04:54.020
The return of Billy Zane

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:59.060
Zane may not be an A-lister headlining giant
movies anymore, but that's not his niche anyway.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:03.789
He's more of a character actor, handsome enough
to play Cads, charismatic villains, and engrossing

00:05:03.789 --> 00:05:04.789
weirdos.

00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:07.669
That said, he still continues to work as often
as he likes.

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:11.779
He's got multiple movies set for release in
the future, including the post-apocalyptic

00:05:11.779 --> 00:05:17.080
sci-fi movie Blue World Order, and the comedy
The Last Party with fellow '90s survivors

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.219
Sharon Stone and Gina Gershon.

00:05:19.219 --> 00:05:23.779
In short, Zane's got versatility, and it continues
to keep him busy, even if we have to try a

00:05:23.779 --> 00:05:26.169
little harder to see him in action these days.

00:05:26.169 --> 00:05:27.589
Plus, he's a cool dude.

00:05:27.589 --> 00:05:29.580
"Listen to your friend Billy Zane.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.129
He's a cool dude."

00:05:32.129 --> 00:05:33.460
Thanks for watching!

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:36.419
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:39.439
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

